  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Trade Mission on Waste Management to Mumbai and New Delhi October 2024</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">India ranks amongst the world&apos;s top 10 countries in generating municipal solid waste owing to rapid urbanisation and economic growth.According to a report by The Energy and Resources Institute (TERI), India generates over 62 million tons of waste annually. Around 43 million tons of the total waste generated is collected, with 12 million tons being treated before disposal, leaving the remaining 31 million tons discarded in waste yards. Most of the waste generated, remains untreated and even unaccounted. Much of this waste leads to a host of environmental and public health issues caused by the release of toxic gasses, smog, and GHG emissions, breeding of pathogens, plastic and heavy metal pollution into water and soil systems.The next big business in India: Waste Commerce. Waste, if managed well, is a highly lucrative source of generating revenue through adoption of circular economy. The Netherlands is a global leader for efficient waste management practices, with strong focus on sustainability, environmental conservation and circular economy. This offers Dutch companies a unique opportunity to deploy waste management solutions in India.IndoreIndore ranked first for the sixth time in a row as the &quot;Cleanest city&quot; of India. The city, through its commendable collaborative efforts, has achieved segregation of waste at source at 100% of its household&apos;s and commercial units, with its citizens playing a significant role in making the city neat and clean. The Swachhata story of Indore is truly transformation through community participation.The Indian Institute of Management-Indore (IIM-Indore) has a collaboration with Indore Municipal Corporation (IMC) to develop a module on solid waste management strategy to help replicate the city&apos;s model of cleanliness in other Indian cities. In 2023, with support from the Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs (MoHUA), IIM-Indore inaugurated its Centre of Excellence (CoE) for waste management and WASH called ANVESHAN. The CoE focuses on capacity building by training mayors, counsellors, and policymakers and aims to boost the implementation of the Indore Swachhata Model, and drive development of sustainable solutions that contribute to a cleaner and healthier country.The IFAT conference in Mumbai is an annual event that brings a strong network of environmental technology solution companies and industry leaders together under one roof.Through this project, a Dutch trade delegation will visit the IFAT conference, which provides an ideal platform to position and promote Dutch expertise and business in India, and to connect Dutch and Indian stakeholders in the waste management sector.</narrative>
  </description>
